<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arzhan kurgans</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th–7th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arzhan kurgans are royal burial mounds in the Uyuk valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuva">Tuva</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. Arzhan 1, of the late ninth or eighth century BCE, is one of the earliest known Scythian-type elite burials, with well over a hundred sacrificed horses. Arzhan 2, of the seventh century BCE and excavated in 2000–2004, held an undisturbed royal couple with some 5,700 gold objects in animal style.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…recognised from the eighth and seventh centuries BCE in cultures such as the Tasmola culture of central Kazakhstan and the early Saka of Zhetysu (Semirechye), while the Aldy-Bel culture of Tuva, whose royal kurgan <b>Arzhan 2</b> held thousands of gold objects, belongs to the same early Scythian horizon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
